A||||8||BAM-BAM|| Is the torque converter lockup solenoid temp sensitive ?||||5||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||10-14-2000||10:27 PM||||  Does anyone know if the torque converter lockup solenoid is temperature sensitive when they start to go bad? How exactly would this problem manefest itself? I'm having a problem with my Grand Prix when it heats up the car jerks and jolts, especialy when slowing, it acts like it won't shift. I had a similar problem a few years ago with my Fiero but I cant remember iff it was temperature related. Any advise is welcome.  <P>  Thanks, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach Springs/KYB shocks and struts<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats, Carpet and headliner! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||5
Z||000001||Vlad||10-14-2000||11:52 PM||||It is temperature related.<P>In most cases you would have no problems at all during first 10-15 minutes after taking off. All bad things related to TCC starts when the car warms up.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||10-15-2000||12:30 AM||||They can be. A good one isn't, but as they age, they can hang up and keep the TCC locked when they get hot.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||10-15-2000||07:34 AM||||When mine went, it was after the transmission warmed up, then it wouldn't unlock..  Try starting out in 3rd gear.  Revs the he11 out and drop it in gear.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-15-2000||11:26 AM||||what year grand prix?<P>unplug the lockup control connector and see if the problem stops. It's usually on the front of GM trannys. actual posittion depends on the tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000005||BAM-BAM||10-15-2000||11:40 AM||||  Ogre,it's a 95GP SE 3.1 auto. I'll try that thanks.<P>  BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach Springs/KYB shocks and struts<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats, Carpet and headliner! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||10-16-2000||09:46 AM||||Mine just acted up on my new 85GT.  I took it out for a test drive and after about 5 miles it wouldn't disengage.  I coasted off the road into a gravel parking lot.  I finally got the wiring plug disconnected (pretty hot place to be putting your hand) but it still would not disengage.  After three tries and about 10 minutes of cooldown time it finally let go.<P>I ordered a new solenoid and gasket.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-16-2000||10:49 AM||||carrol, that's sounds about normal. The connector won't make it disengage once it's stuck. It will keep it from activating and getting restuck.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||10-16-2000||02:25 PM||||Theogre,<P>I guess I was lucky it did un-stick itself.<P>What took the longest was getting the courage to stick my hand down near the y-pipe to unplug the solenoid while the engine was hot.  Being on a test drive less than a mile from home I didn't pack any tools.  Not even a screwdriver.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
